{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The execution date in the opening sentence is illegible and self-contradictory (\"made on / Oo} 24) OO , 2000\"), so the date the Trust was created is not fixed by the words used. Every other document dates the Declaration October 24, 2000, but this provision does not say so legibly.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.12}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Recital (c) says the Trustee \"commits to limiting participation in the Trust to less than 100 Shareholders,\" while operative Section 4.4 says the Trust shall not have \"more than 100 Shareholders\" — the recital and the operative term set different ceilings. A court would have to decide whether the recital's commitment controls the operative section.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c004", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c005", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The definition cross-refers \"Covered Person\" to Article Nine, Section 9.2, but Section 9.2 is \"Trustee Action; Expert Advice; No Bond or Surety\" and contains no such definition; the term is actually defined in Article Eight, Section 8.1. The mis-citation leaves the defined class of protected persons to be fixed by construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Majority Shareholder Vote\" is defined, but Section 4.8 denies Shareholders any right to vote and Section 2.2(b) uses a two-thirds vote rather than a majority, so the defined term has no operative field. A court would have to decide whether the definition presupposes voting rights the instrument elsewhere withholds.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The definition fixes \"Net Asset Value\" at the Series level, while Sections 4.5, 5.2 and 5.4 all operate on \"Net Asset Value per Share,\" a figure this definition does not supply. The per-Share measure a redeeming Shareholder is entitled to therefore rests on an undefined derivative of the defined term.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Outstanding Shares\" excludes redeemed Shares held in treasury, but Section 5.2 provides that upon redemption Shares \"shall not be canceled\" and may be reissued, leaving unclear whether redeemed Shares drop out of the count. That count drives the two-thirds removal threshold in Section 2.2(b) and the 100-Shareholder limit in Section 4.4.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.12}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Shareholder\" is limited to a \"record owner of Outstanding Shares,\" yet Section 4.4 counts beneficial owners holding through flow-through entities as Shareholders for the 100-holder limit. The two treatments of who is a Shareholder cannot both be applied consistently.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"Shares\" are defined as \"transferable units of interest,\" which cannot be squared with Section 4.9, under which the beneficial interest \"shall not be transferable and any purported transfer shall be void.\" A court would have to decide which characterization governs the alienability of a Shareholder's interest.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The definition is circular — \"'Trust' means the Trust, created hereby\" — and so supplies no content beyond the preamble. Whether \"Trust\" includes the separate Series must be worked out from Article Four instead.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.10}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The definition contemplates a plurality of Trustees \"acting collectively,\" while Section 2.2(a) provides that \"[t]here shall be one trustee.\" The plural apparatus of the definition has no application to the single corporate Trustee actually serving.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.11}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c017", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c018", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The conclusive-determination sentence (\"[a]ny determination made by the Trustee in good faith as to what is in the interests of the Trust shall be conclusive\") cannot be squared with Section 3.3, which subjects the Trustee to an ERISA prudent-person standard, and with Section 8.5, which preserves ERISA liability. Good faith alone would displace a duty the instrument elsewhere imposes.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c019", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"There shall be one trustee, except as the Trustee may determine otherwise\" cannot be squared with Sections 2.3 and 2.4, which operate through \"the remaining Trustee or Trustees\" and \"a majority of the Trustees.\" With a sole trustee those mechanisms have nothing to work on.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c020", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Removal of the Trustee \"by a vote of at least two-thirds of the Outstanding Shares at a meeting of the Shareholders\" is flatly contrary to Section 4.8, which says Shareholders have no right \"to appoint, select, vote for or remove the Trustee.\" The same conflict attends the clause's reference to holding office \"until its successor is elected.\"", "type": 2, "prob": 0.42}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c021", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Succession depends on \"the remaining Trustee or Trustees\" appointing a replacement, but under Section 2.2(a) there is only one trustee, so resignation or removal of that sole Trustee leaves no one empowered to fill the vacancy. The provision supplies no fallback mechanism.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c022", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The section vests \"all the powers hereunder\" in \"the remaining Trustees\" during a vacancy and allows delegation \"to any other Trustee or Trustees,\" neither of which can operate under the single-trustee structure of Section 2.2(a). Who holds the powers when the sole Trustee's office is vacant is left unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c023", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The closing sentence is garbled and carries a broken cross-reference (\"each Shareholder shall have as provided in Article Four, 1.2 proportionate undivided beneficial interest\"), so the nature and measure of a Shareholder's interest cannot be read off the text. The stray \"1.2\" corresponds to no section of this Declaration.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c024", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Compensation \"as may be fixed from time to time by the Trustee\" itself sits against the last sentence of Section 6.1, which bars retention of the Trustee or its affiliates \"for additional compensation beyond that disclosed and agreed to in the various written agreements entered into between Shareholders and the Trustee.\" Whether the Trustee can unilaterally set fees beyond the securities lending agreement's agreed split is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c025", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Clause (h) makes the power to sell or exchange assets \"subject to Article Eleven, Section 11.4,\" but this Declaration ends at Article Nine and contains no Section 11.4; clause (l) likewise trails off (\"[t]o the full extent permitted by Section 12-\") and points liabilities to \"Article Four, Section 4.5,\" which concerns investments rather than Series liabilities. The limits on the enumerated powers thus cannot be located in the instrument.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35},
    {"issue": "The closing \"[n]otwithstanding\" paragraph requires the Trustee to comply with the affiliated-transaction prohibitions of 1940 Act Section 17(a), (d) and (e) and the leverage prohibitions of Section 18 as if registered, which cannot be squared with Section 3.2's permission to deal with affiliates and clause (o)'s power to borrow and pledge Trust assets. A court would have to decide which of the two governs an affiliated or leveraged transaction.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.32}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c026", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The permission to buy from, sell to and lend Trust assets to the Trustee, its officers, its investment adviser and transfer agent cannot be squared with the final paragraph of Section 3.1, which requires the Trustee to comply with the 1940 Act Section 17(a), (d) and (e) prohibitions on affiliated transactions as if the Trust were registered. The two provisions give opposite answers on self-dealing.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c027", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The ERISA prudent-person standard of care imposed here cannot be squared with Section 8.1, which exculpates the Trustee for everything short of bad faith, gross negligence, willful misconduct or reckless disregard, or with Section 8.4's replacement of duties existing at law or equity. Section 8.5 saves only Part 4 ERISA duties, so the standard's reach as to non-ERISA Shareholders is contested.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.38}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c028", "issues": [
