{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c041", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The redemption right is keyed to \"the close of business on each Business Day,\" but \"Business Day\" is nowhere defined in Article One or elsewhere in this Declaration, even though the Confidential Memoranda expressly say \"Business Day (as defined in the Declaration of Trust).\" The frequency and timing of the redemption right therefore rest on an undefined term.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32},
    {"issue": "The suspension power — \"a period not exceeding seven days\" in the Trustee's judgment — cannot be squared with the Confidential Memorandum for the CI Term Trust, which conditions redemption on seven business days' notice and reserves a right to postpone payment or suspend redemptions \"in certain circumstances as specified in the Declaration of Trust.\" The two documents describe different redemption and suspension mechanics.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c042", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Net Asset Value is to be determined \"in a manner consistent with the 1940 Act,\" but the Trust is unregistered and the Act supplies several valuation regimes (market value versus Rule 2a-7 amortized cost), so the phrase fixes no method. The Confidential Memoranda instead say assets \"will be valued each Business Day at their then current market value.\"", "type": 1, "prob": 0.25}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c043", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This section is expressly premised on the Trustee \"postpon[ing] payment of the redemption price\" \"as referred to in Section 5.2,\" but Section 5.2 contains no postponement power — only a suspension of the right of redemption for not more than seven days. The trigger for suspension therefore points to something Section 5.2 does not say.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c044", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The bar on retaining the Trustee or its affiliates \"for additional compensation beyond that disclosed and agreed to in the various written agreements entered into between Shareholders and the Trustee\" cannot be squared with Section 2.6 and Section 3.1(k), which let the Trustee fix and pay itself compensation, or with the Confidential Memoranda, under which Wells Fargo appointed itself investment manager and may take fees it determines to be reasonable. Which regime limits the Trustee's fees is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c045", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"[C]ontracts for such other services deemed necessary or appropriate by the Trustee\" is an open-ended catch-all appended to the specific categories of transfer agency, accounting and administration, with no ejusdem generis limit stated. Its reach against the enumerated specifics would have to be construed.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.12}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c046", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This section allows \"broker-dealers, brokers, banks or trust companies\" to serve as custodian, whereas Section 3.1(d) confines custodians to banks, trust companies, members of a national securities exchange \"or other entities permitted by the Commission to serve as such.\" The two lists of eligible custodians do not match.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c047", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Validating affiliate contracts so long as they are \"reasonable and fair\" cannot be squared with the closing paragraph of Section 3.1, which requires compliance with the 1940 Act Section 17(a), (d) and (e) prohibitions on affiliated transactions as if the Trust were registered. A self-dealing contract could satisfy one and violate the other.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c048", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The Article opens \"Subject to Article Four, Section 4.4,\" but Section 4.4 is the publicly-traded-partnership/100-Shareholder provision and says nothing about expenses; the qualification points to a section that cannot qualify it. What limits the expense obligation is therefore indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c049", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The exculpation, which imposes liability only for \"bad faith, gross negligence, willful misconduct or reckless disregard,\" cannot be squared with the Securities Lending Agreement, under which \"[t]he Bank assumes the risk of loss arising from negligent and fraudulent operation of its Securities Lending Program,\" or with the Confidential Memoranda, which preserve liability for simple \"negligence.\" Whether ordinary negligence is actionable turns on which document governs.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.55},
    {"issue": "The same limitation cannot be squared with Section 3.3, which binds the Trustee to the ERISA prudent-person standard, and with Section 8.5, which withholds Article Eight's protection for Part 4 ERISA duties. The scope of the exculpation as against a fiduciary duty imposed elsewhere in the instrument is contested.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c050", "issues": [
    {"issue": "Mandatory indemnification \"to the fullest extent permitted under the Maryland Act\" for \"all liabilities and expenses\" incurred by virtue of being a Covered Person carries no exclusion for bad faith, gross negligence or willful misconduct, unlike Section 8.1 and unlike the Confidential Memoranda's carve-outs. Whether a Covered Person held liable under Section 8.1 may nonetheless be indemnified is unresolved.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c051", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c052", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The closing proviso relieves the Trust of paying \"the expenses of any agent acting pursuant to a written contract with the Trust, except to the extent required by such contract,\" which cuts against Section 8.2(a)'s mandatory indemnification of every Covered Person, a class that includes agents. Which of the two governs an agent's advancement claim is left open.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c053", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The subsection speaks of \"[a]ny repeal or modification of this Article Seven,\" but it sits in Article Eight; Article Seven is the expenses article. The anti-retroactivity protection therefore names the wrong article on its face.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c054", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The trigger — personal liability \"solely by reason of his or her being ... a Shareholder and not because of his or her acts or omissions or for some other reason\" — is internally contradictory, since \"or for some other reason\" negates the \"solely\" it qualifies. When indemnity is owed cannot be read off the sentence.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.18}
